/**
@mainpage Schrödinger's Smoke

@section intro Overview

A real-time implementation of the Incompressible Schrödinger Flow (ISF) algorithm
from Chern, Knöppel, Pinkall, Schröder, Weißmann (SIGGRAPH 2016).  The fluid state
is a two-component complex wave function ψ : M → C² on a periodic 3D lattice.
Velocity is recovered from the edge 1-form η = ℏ · arg⟨ψ_v, ψ_w⟩.  Vorticity
concentrates into quantized filaments of strength 2πℏ, producing leapfrogging
rings, reconnection, and turbulent mixing without Lagrangian tracking.

@section algo Algorithm (per time step)

The solver implements Algorithm 1 of the paper:

1. **Schrödinger integration** (@ref isf::schrodinger_step) — FFT spectral
   propagator using the continuous Laplacian eigenvalues (Eq. 18).
2. **Normalization** (@ref isf::normalize) — pointwise rescale to |ψ|² = 1.
3. **Pressure projection** (@ref isf::pressure_project) — enforce divergence-free
   velocity via a discrete Poisson solve (FFT, discrete Laplacian Eq. 17).
4. **Velocity recovery** (@ref isf::recover_velocity) — edge arg averaging to
   vertex-centered 3-vectors for particle advection.

Step 4 has a fast variant @ref isf::recover_velocity_from_workspace that derives
the post-correction velocity algebraically (η' = η − ∇q) from the cached pressure
workspace, eliminating redundant inner-product computation (~25% speedup at 128³).

@section init Initial conditions

- **Vortex ring** (@ref isf::init_vortex_ring_z) — slab construction from Sec. 3.1
  of the paper.  A 2π phase winding across a disk-shaped slab creates a single
  quantized vortex filament along the disk boundary.
- **Colliding spheres** (@ref isf::init_two_velocity_balls) — two ball-shaped
  regions initialized with opposing velocities via iterated constraint projection
  (Algorithm 4, Sec. 3.2).

@section classes Key classes

- @ref isf::Grid3D — periodic 3D lattice storing the two-component wave function
  ψ = (ψ₁, ψ₂) as separate complex arrays in row-major (x-fastest) layout.
- @ref isf::PressureWorkspace — reusable scratch buffers for pressure projection
  (edge 1-forms, divergence, Poisson potential, FFT scratch).  Also supplies the
  cached data for the fast velocity recovery path.
- @ref isf::VelocityField — vertex-centered velocity (vx, vy, vz) in the same
  layout as the grid.
- @ref viz::ParticleSystem — structure-of-arrays particle cloud with optional
  per-particle colour for the colliding-spheres demo.
- @ref viz::ParticleRenderer — OpenGL 4.1 additive point-sprite renderer.
- @ref viz::OrbitCamera — mouse-controlled orbit camera.
- @ref viz::GifRecorder — captures rendered frames to animated GIF via msf_gif.

@section perf Performance

Benchmarked on Apple Silicon (single socket), 150k tracer particles:

| Resolution | ms / frame | FPS |
|------------|-----------|-----|
| 64³        | 9 ms      | 109 |
| 96³        | 22 ms     | 45  |
| 128³       | 57 ms     | 18  |
| 160³       | 93 ms     | 11  |

@section deps Dependencies

- C++20 compiler
- CMake ≥ 3.20
- GLFW 3, GLM (system packages)
- OpenMP (optional, for parallel solver loops)
- pocketfft (vendored, header-only)
- Dear ImGui (fetched by CMake)
- Catch2 v3 (fetched by CMake)
- msf_gif (vendored, header-only)
*/
